      "topic": "Behavior",
      "content": "<p>Kneading is the rhythmic pushing of the front paws against a soft surface. Many adult cats do it when relaxed, sleepy, or preparing to settle. In most cases it is a normal behavior.</p>\n<h2>The Most Useful Question: What Happens With the Kneading?</h2>\n<p>A relaxed cat that kneads briefly and then lies down is showing a different pattern from a cat that becomes agitated, chews fabric intensely, or swallows threads or pieces of blanket.</p>\n<h2>Why Soft Fabric Is Appealing</h2>\n<p>Blankets provide resistance under the paws and can become part of a familiar resting routine. Kneading also has roots in kitten nursing behavior, and many cats continue the motor pattern into adulthood.</p>\n<h2>Manage Sharp Claws Without Punishing the Behavior</h2>\n<ul><li>Place a thick washable blanket between paws and skin.</li><li>Keep nail tips appropriately maintained.</li><li>Move the cat gently to a designated soft surface if needed.</li><li>Avoid punishment.</li></ul>\n<h2>Fabric Chewing Changes the Risk</h2>\n<p>Some cats mouth fabric while kneading. Brief suckling may be a longstanding habit, but swallowing wool, thread, string, or pieces of fabric can create gastrointestinal danger. Remove damaged fabric and discuss persistent ingestion of non-food material with a veterinarian.</p>\n<h2>When Kneading Deserves More Attention</h2>\n<p>Seek veterinary or behavior advice when kneading becomes sudden and compulsive, causes paw injury, is paired with marked distress, or includes fabric ingestion.</p>",
