      "slug": "why-does-my-cat-rub-against-my-legs",
      "title": "Why Does My Cat Rub Against My Legs? Greeting, Scent, and Safer Ways to Respond",
      "metaDescription": "Learn why cats rub against legs, how greeting, scent and learned routines contribute, and how to prevent trips without discouraging normal social contact.",
      "image": "/images/cats/why-does-my-cat-rub-against-my-legs.webp",
      "alt": "Friendly cat rubbing gently against its owner's legs",
      "summary": "Leg rubbing is usually normal social behavior, but the sequence before and after the rub can show whether your cat is greeting you, requesting something, or both.",
      "category": "Cats",
      "primaryKeyword": "why does my cat rub against my legs",
      "secondaryKeywords": [
        "cat rubbing on owner",
        "cat head bunting",
        "cat scent marking people",
        "cat greeting behavior"
      ],
      "author": "Virixoo Editorial Team",
      "datePublished": "2026-08-14",
      "dateModified": "2026-11-02",
      "topic": "Behavior",
      "content": "<p>A cat weaving around your ankles or rubbing its face and body along your legs is usually combining social contact, scent communication, and routine. The behavior often appears when a familiar person comes home, near meal times, or when the cat wants interaction.</p>\n<h2>Watch Where the Cat Goes Next</h2>\n<ul><li>If the cat rubs, tail up, then stays with you, the interaction may be mostly social.</li><li>If it rubs and immediately walks toward food, it may also be a learned request.</li><li>If it rubs near a door and waits there, access or routine may be involved.</li></ul>\n<h2>Scent Is Part of Normal Feline Communication</h2>\n<p>Cats have scent glands around the face and body, and rubbing can transfer familiar scent. It is more useful to think of this as normal communication than as proof that the cat is trying to “own” a person.</p>\n<h2>Prevent Trips Without Discouraging Contact</h2>\n<p>When a cat weaves directly between your feet, pause rather than stepping over it. You can reward a safer greeting location beside a mat, stool, or perch.</p>\n<h2>When Rubbing Is Not Just a Greeting</h2>\n<p>Sudden intense rubbing focused on one side of the face can occur with eye, ear, skin, or dental discomfort. A major new increase in clinginess with appetite change, pain, weakness, confusion, or persistent vocalization also deserves attention.</p>",
